From: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: sc8280xp: raise the WSA digital volume ceiling to +6 dB

NOT FOR UPSTREAM -- this deliberately trades away part of a safety margin
that upstream put there on purpose. It is here because on this particular
machine the margin costs more than it buys.

sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c caps the speaker digital volume at -3 dB:

	/*
	 * Set limit of -3 dB on Digital Volume and 0 dB on PA Volume
	 * to reduce the risk of speaker damage until we have active
	 * speaker protection in place.
	 */
	snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 81);

On a Huawei MateBook E Go the result is speakers most users describe as too
quiet to be usable.

What made this hard to find is that the cap is invisible from userspace
except as a number: the four WSA digital volume controls are declared
identically in lpass-wsa-macro.c

	SOC_SINGLE_S8_TLV("WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", CDC_WSA_RX0_RX_VOL_CTL,
			  -84, 40, digital_gain),
	SOC_SINGLE_S8_TLV("WSA_RX0_MIX Digital Volume", ...,
			  -84, 40, digital_gain),

yet the device reports a range of 0..81 for the first and 0..124 for the
second. snd_soc_limit_volume() sets platform_max on two of the four, and
nothing else in the stack hints at why.

Measurement on the device, playing a 440 Hz tone through tinyplay and
recording it with the built-in microphone (Goertzel at 440 Hz; silence reads
-99.4 dBFS against -23.2 dBFS with the tone, so the measurement has 76 dB of
headroom):

  WSA_RX Digital Volume  81 -> 61 -> 41   =  -23.2 -> -40.3 -> -50.0 dBFS
  WSA_RX_MIX Digital Volume 84 -> 124     =  no change (not in the path)
  SpkrLeft/Right PA Volume  12 -> 6 -> 1  =  no change
  BOOST port switch off -> on             =  no change
  compander switch on -> off              =  no change

So this control is the only working gain in the speaker path, and it is
pinned 3 dB below unity while the control itself goes to +40 dB.

Raise the ceiling to +6 dB. The scale is value - 84 dB, so 90 is +6 dB and
the stock 81 is exactly the -3 dB the upstream comment describes.

The PA Volume limits are left alone: measurement says that control does not
affect output on this machine at all, so relaxing it would only add risk.

Device-specific justification for accepting the reduced margin: these are the
small speakers of a fanless 12.35" tablet, the WSA883x compander and VISENSE
feedback are both enabled, and the factory calibration data this SoC would
normally load does not exist on a machine whose Windows install has been
erased -- so the upstream default is not protecting anything it could not
also protect at +6 dB.

--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c
@@ -39,12 +39,31 @@ static int sc8280xp_snd_init(struct snd_soc_pcm_runtime *rtd)
 	case WSA_CODEC_DMA_RX_0:
 	case WSA_CODEC_DMA_RX_1:
 		/*
-		 * Set limit of -3 dB on Digital Volume and 0 dB on PA Volume
-		 * to reduce the risk of speaker damage until we have active
-		 * speaker protection in place.
+		 * Upstream limits this to -3 dB "to reduce the risk of
+		 * speaker damage until we have active speaker protection
+		 * in place". On the Huawei MateBook E Go that margin makes
+		 * the speakers too quiet to be usable, and measurement
+		 * shows this control is the only working gain in the
+		 * speaker path: stepping it 81 -> 61 -> 41 moves the
+		 * acoustic output -17.1 dB and -26.9 dB, while PA Volume,
+		 * the BOOST port switch and the compander switch change
+		 * nothing at all.
+		 *
+		 * Raise the ceiling to +6 dB. The control scale is
+		 * value - 84 dB, so 90 is +6 dB and the stock 81 is the
+		 * -3 dB the comment above refers to.
+		 *
+		 * This trades away part of a deliberate safety margin, so
+		 * it is a local change and not something to send upstream.
+		 * The device-specific justification: these are the small
+		 * speakers of a fanless 12.35" tablet, the compander and
+		 * VISENSE feedback are both enabled, and the factory
+		 * calibration this SoC would normally use died with the
+		 * Windows install, so upstream cannot assume protection
+		 * either way.
 		 */
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 81);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 90);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 90);
 		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrLeft PA Volume", 17);
 		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrRight PA Volume", 17);
 		break;
